  "cyA1Title": "What Is a Menstrual Cycle?",
  "cyA1Sub": "The complete beginner's guide to understanding your body",
  "cyA1S1H": "What is actually happening?",
  "cyA1S1P1": "Every month, your body goes through a series of changes designed to prepare for a possible pregnancy. This sequence of events is called the menstrual cycle. It involves your brain, your ovaries, your uterus, and a carefully timed series of hormonal signals that all work together in a coordinated rhythm.",
  "cyA1S1P2": "The menstrual cycle is a series of natural changes in hormone production and the structures of the uterus and ovaries of the female reproductive system. The ovarian cycle controls the production and release of eggs, and the uterine cycle governs the preparation and maintenance of the lining of the uterus to receive an embryo. These two cycles run concurrently and are coordinated with each other.",
  "cyA1S1P3": "In simple terms: your ovaries grow and release an egg. Your uterus builds up a thick, soft lining in case that egg gets fertilised. If it doesn't, the lining sheds. That shedding is your period. Then the whole process starts again.",
  "cyA1S2H": "The organs involved",
  "cyA1S2P1": "Understanding your cycle starts with knowing which parts of your body are involved:",
  "cyA1S2B1T": "The ovaries",
  "cyA1S2B1B": "Two small, almond-shaped organs on either side of your uterus. They store your eggs and produce the hormones estrogen and progesterone. You are born with all the eggs you will ever have — roughly 1 to 2 million at birth, which reduces to around 300,000 by puberty.",
  "cyA1S2B2T": "The uterus",
  "cyA1S2B2B": "A pear-shaped muscular organ where a baby grows during pregnancy. Its inner lining, called the endometrium, builds up and sheds every cycle.",
  "cyA1S2B3T": "The fallopian tubes",
  "cyA1S2B3B": "Two narrow tubes connecting the ovaries to the uterus. When an egg is released, it travels down the fallopian tube toward the uterus.",
  "cyA1S2B4T": "The hypothalamus and pituitary gland",
  "cyA1S2B4B": "Located in your brain. These send out the hormonal signals that start and control the entire cycle.",
  "cyA1S3H": "When does it all begin?",
  "cyA1S3P1": "Menarche — the first menstrual period — typically occurs between the ages of 10 and 16, with the average age of onset being 12.4 years.",
  "cyA1S3P2": "Another way to predict when your period will come is to think back to when breast development began — menarche usually happens about 2 to 2.5 years after breasts start developing.",
  "cyA1S3P3": "The age varies widely from person to person and is influenced by genetics, body composition, nutrition, and general health. People commonly get their periods at around the same time their mother did. Getting your first period any time between ages 9 and 15 is considered within the normal range.",
  "cyA1S4H": "How long is a normal cycle?",
  "cyA1S4P1": "This is where a lot of confusion starts. Most people have heard that a cycle is 28 days. That number is an average — not a rule.",
  "cyA1S4P2": "For teenagers, a normal menstrual cycle can be anywhere between 21 and 45 days. The average menstrual cycle length is approximately 28 days.",
  "cyA1S4P3": "A large-scale real-world study published in npj Digital Medicine analysed data from over 600,000 cycles and found that the mean cycle length across ovulatory cycles was 29.3 days, with a mean follicular phase length of 16.9 days and a mean luteal phase length of 12.4 days.",
  "cyA1S4P4": "Research from the Apple Women's Health Study — one of the largest studies of its kind, conducted by Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health — analysed 165,668 cycles across 12,608 participants and found that cycle variability is considerably higher — by 46% — among those aged under 20 compared to those aged 35 to 39. In other words, irregular cycles are the norm for teenagers, not the exception.",
  "cyA1S5H": "How long does a period last?",
  "cyA1S5P1": "According to the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO), normal menstrual cycles should have consistent frequency, regularity, duration, and volume of flow.",
  "cyA1S5P2": "A period typically lasts between 3 and 7 days, though anywhere in that range is normal. The amount of blood lost during a typical period is around 30 to 80 mL — roughly 2 to 6 tablespoons.",
  "cyA1S6H": "Why are teenage cycles so irregular?",
  "cyA1S6P1": "In the first 1 to 2 years following your first period, it is very common and normal to have irregular cycles. In fact, in the first year after your first period, up to 80% of your menstrual cycles may be anovulatory — meaning no egg is released.",
  "cyA1S6P2": "This happens because the hormonal communication system between your brain and ovaries — called the HPO axis — is still maturing. It takes time for this system to find its rhythm. During the first two years following menarche, ovulation is absent in around half of cycles. Five years after menarche, ovulation occurs in around 75% of cycles.",
  "cyA1S6P3": "A 2024 study published in ScienceDirect, analysing 38,916 cycles from 6,486 adolescents aged 13–18 using the Clue app, found that individuals less than 1 year post-menarche had a 2.6 times higher odds of having a highly variable cycle and 5 times higher odds of short cycles compared to those further along in their reproductive development.",
  "cyA1S6KP1": "Irregular cycles in your teens are biological, not a sign something is wrong.",
  "cyA1S6KP2": "It can take 2 to 5 years after your first period for cycles to stabilise.",
  "cyA1S6KP3": "The 28-day average applies to adults, not teenagers.",
  "cyA1S6KP4": "Your cycle length may vary by several days from month to month and that is completely normal.",
  "cyA1S7H": "What does \"Day 1\" mean?",
  "cyA1S7P1": "When discussing timing within the menstrual cycle, the first day of heavy menstrual flow is considered Day 1. This is the standard used by doctors and researchers worldwide. Every cycle is measured from Day 1 of one period to Day 1 of the next.",
  "cyA1S8H": "When should you talk to a doctor?",
  "cyA1S8P1": "Most cycle irregularities in teenagers are normal. However, there are specific situations where it is worth speaking to a doctor or a trusted adult:",
  "cyA1S8L1": "Your first period has not arrived by age 15.",
  "cyA1S8L2": "Your periods stop for 3 or more months in a row and you are not pregnant.",
  "cyA1S8L3": "Your cycle is consistently shorter than 21 days or longer than 45 days.",
  "cyA1S8L4": "Your period lasts longer than 7 days regularly.",
  "cyA1S8L5": "You are soaking through a pad or tampon in under 2 hours.",
  "cyA1S8L6": "Your periods cause pain severe enough to miss school or daily activities.",
  "cyA1GraphTitle": "Average cycle length by age group — Apple Women's Health Study, 2023 (n = 165,668 cycles)",
  "cyA1GraphUnder20": "Under\n20",
  "cyA1GraphYourAge": "Your age group",
  "cyA1GraphOtherAge": "Other age groups",
  "cyA1GraphCaption": "Cycles are longest and most variable in the teenage years and gradually shorten and stabilise into the late twenties and thirties.",
  "cyA2Title": "The Four Phases of Your Cycle",
  "cyA2Sub": "What your body is doing every single day of the month",
  "cyA2OvH": "Overview",
  "cyA2OvP1": "Most people think of their menstrual cycle as just their period — a few uncomfortable days every month. But your period is only one of four distinct phases that your body moves through every single cycle. Each phase has its own hormonal environment, its own physical changes, and its own emotional signature.",
  "cyA2OvP2": "The menstrual cycle comprises two distinct cycles — one within the ovary and another within the endometrium. The phases of the ovarian cycle include the follicular phase, ovulation, and the luteal phase, while the endometrial cycle consists of the proliferative phase, the secretory phase, and the menstrual phase. These phases are coordinated with each other and run simultaneously.",
  "cyA2OvP3": "In everyday language, these are grouped into four phases: menstrual, follicular, ovulation, and the luteal phase. Here is what happens in each one.",
  "cyA2P1H": "Phase 1 — The Menstrual Phase",
  "cyA2P1Meta": "Days 1 to 3–7  |  Hormone profile: Estrogen low, progesterone low",
  "cyA2P1P1": "This is Day 1. The first day of your period is officially the first day of your entire cycle — not the end of it.",
  "cyA2P1P2": "The menstrual phase starts with the shedding of the uterine lining, which occurs when a drop in estrogen and progesterone signals the uterus to shed its endometrial lining. The average blood loss during a period is around 2 to 3 tablespoons.",
  "cyA2P1P3": "How long a period lasts varies by person, but most periods last 3 to 7 days, with 5 to 6 days being most common. If your period consistently lasts longer than 8 days or is very heavy, consult your healthcare provider.",
  "cyA2PhysLabel": "What you might feel physically:",
  "cyA2P1Phys1": "Cramping in the lower abdomen and back — caused by prostaglandins, chemicals that trigger uterine contractions.",
  "cyA2P1Phys2": "Fatigue and low energy — your body is doing real physiological work.",
  "cyA2P1Phys3": "Flow that is heavier at the start and lighter toward the end.",
  "cyA2EmoLabel": "What you might feel emotionally:",
  "cyA2P1Emo1": "Lower mood and reduced motivation as both estrogen and progesterone are at their lowest point.",
  "cyA2P1Emo2": "Increased sensitivity and a desire to rest and withdraw.",
  "cyA2P2H": "Phase 2 — The Follicular Phase",
  "cyA2P2Meta": "Days 1 to ~14  |  Hormone profile: Estrogen rising, FSH active",
  "cyA2P2P1": "The follicular phase begins on the same day as your period and runs until ovulation. The name comes from follicles: tiny fluid-filled sacs in your ovaries, each containing an immature egg.",
  "cyA2P2P2": "This phase starts when the brain releases follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH). This stimulates the ovaries to produce around 5 to 20 small follicles. Only the healthiest egg will eventually mature — the rest are reabsorbed. The average follicular phase lasts about 16 days, ranging from 11 to 27 days depending on the cycle.",
  "cyA2P2P3": "The development of the dominant follicle happens in three stages: recruitment (days 1 to 4), selection (days 5 to 7), and dominance (from day 8 onward). By cycle day 8, one follicle exerts dominance by promoting its own growth and suppressing others.",
  "cyA2P2P4": "A groundbreaking 2024 study found that during the pre-ovulatory phase (the end of the follicular phase), brain network connectivity and complexity are at their highest. You're not just having a good week by chance — your brain is operating in its most responsive state.",
  "cyA2P2KeyT": "Key point",
  "cyA2P2KeyB": "The length of this phase varies most between individuals. The luteal phase is usually stable at 14 days — so variability in overall cycle length comes almost entirely from the follicular phase.",
  "cyA2P3H": "Phase 3 — Ovulation",
  "cyA2P3Meta": "Day ~14  |  Hormone profile: LH surge, estrogen peaks then drops",
  "cyA2P3P1": "Ovulation is a single event, not a phase — it lasts only 12 to 24 hours. But it is the central event of the entire cycle. Everything before it builds toward it, and everything after is a response to it.",
  "cyA2P3P2": "Ovulation typically occurs approximately 36 to 44 hours after the onset of the LH surge. At the end of ovulation, levels of estradiol decrease. Cervical changes result in increased, watery cervical mucus to facilitate sperm entry.",
  "cyA2P3P3": "In the middle of the cycle, a surge of luteinizing hormone triggers the release of a mature egg from the dominant follicle in one of the ovaries. The egg travels down the fallopian tube where it stays for 12 to 24 hours.",
  "cyA2P3P4": "Only about 13% of people have exactly 28-day cycles, so significant variation in ovulation timing is completely normal.",
  "cyA2P4H": "Phase 4 — The Luteal Phase",
  "cyA2P4Meta": "Days ~15 to 28  |  Hormone profile: Progesterone dominant",
  "cyA2P4P1": "After ovulation, the follicle that released the egg transforms into a temporary gland called the corpus luteum, which begins producing progesterone. This is the phase most people feel the most.",
  "cyA2P4P2": "The empty follicle produces progesterone and some estrogen to support a potential pregnancy. If no pregnancy occurs, it breaks down after about 9 to 11 days. The luteal phase often lasts about 14 days but can range between 9 and 16 days.",
  "cyA2P4P3": "PMS is likely influenced by the action of progesterone on neurotransmitters including GABA, serotonin, and dopamine. Clinical trials show that serotonin levels shift significantly during this phase, linking PMS to mood changes.",
  "cyA2P4P4": "A 2024 study published in Nature Neuroscience found measurable structural changes in the brain during the luteal phase. Luteal phase symptoms aren't \"all in your head\" — they're rooted in real neurobiological changes driven by hormones.",
  "cyA2P4P5": "Food cravings, especially for carbohydrates and sugar, are common as progesterone and serotonin fluctuations drive appetite during this phase.",
  "cyA2P4P6": "Large surveys show up to 90% of people who menstruate experience at least one PMS symptom like anger, irritability, or bloating.",
  "cyA2S8H": "When should you talk to a doctor?",
  "cyA2S8L1": "Your periods are consistently causing pain severe enough to miss school or daily activities.",
  "cyA2S8L2": "Luteal phase mood symptoms are significantly affecting your relationships or mental health.",
  "cyA2S8L3": "You experience no recognisable phase pattern (no energy shifts or mucus changes).",
  "cyA2S8P1": "In some cases, ovulation may not occur, resulting in anovulatory cycles. These are common in the first 12 to 18 months after the first period. If you are well past your first year and symptoms suggest you aren't ovulating, a doctor can investigate.",
  "cyA2GraphTitle": "Hormone levels across a 28-day menstrual cycle",
  "cyA2GraphMenstrual": "Menstrual",
  "cyA2GraphFollicular": "Follicular",
  "cyA2GraphOv": "Ov.",
  "cyA2GraphLuteal": "Luteal",
  "cyA2GraphEstrogen": "Estrogen",
  "cyA2GraphProgesterone": "Progesterone",
  "cyA2GraphLH": "LH surge",
  "cyA2GraphCaption": "Estrogen peaks just before ovulation, the LH surge triggers egg release, then progesterone takes over for the luteal phase.",
  "cyA3Title": "Hormones and Your Cycle",
  "cyA3Sub": "What estrogen, progesterone, LH, and FSH actually do — and why they affect your entire life",
  "cyA3S1H": "Why hormones matter beyond reproduction",
  "cyA3S1P1": "Most people think of reproductive hormones as purely physical — they control your period, full stop. The reality is far more interesting. The hormones that drive your menstrual cycle are neuroactive steroids: they cross the blood-brain barrier, act directly on the brain, and shape your mood, memory, motivation, sleep, appetite, and pain sensitivity throughout the month.",
  "cyA3S1P2": "The menstrual cycle is an intricate biological process governed by hormonal changes that affect different facets of the female reproductive system — but also broad effects on psychology, cognition, and emotional experience across each phase.",
  "cyA3S1P3": "There are four primary hormones to understand. Two are produced in the brain. Two are produced in the ovaries. Together they form a continuous feedback loop that runs without stopping from your first period to your last.",
  "cyA3S2H": "The hormonal command chain",
  "cyA3S2P1": "Before getting to the four main hormones, it helps to understand that the system has a clear hierarchy. It starts in the brain, not the ovaries.",
  "cyA3S2P2": "Hormonal regulation begins in the hypothalamus, where gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) is secreted in a pulsatile fashion starting at puberty. GnRH is transported to the anterior pituitary, where it signals the pituitary gland to release follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinizing hormone (LH). FSH and LH then travel through the bloodstream to the ovaries, stimulating the production of sex steroid hormones from follicular cells.",
  "cyA3S2P3": "In plain terms: your brain sends a signal, which tells your pituitary gland to release two hormones, which travel to your ovaries, which produce estrogen and progesterone, which feed back to the brain to regulate the next signal. This loop repeats every cycle.",
  "cyA3FshH": "Hormone 1 — FSH (Follicle-Stimulating Hormone)",
  "cyA3FshMeta": "Produced by: The pituitary gland, located at the base of the brain\nPrimary role: Stimulating egg development",
  "cyA3FshP1": "FSH's main function is to help regulate the menstrual cycle. Specifically, FSH stimulates follicles on the ovary to grow and prepare the eggs for ovulation. As the follicles increase in size, they begin to release estrogen and a low level of progesterone into the bloodstream.",
  "cyA3FshP2": "FSH is highest at the very start of your cycle, when estrogen is at its lowest. This is the brain's response to the hormonal drop at the end of the previous cycle — it detects low estrogen and sends FSH to restart follicle development.",
  "cyA3FshP3": "One follicle will soon begin to grow faster than the others. This is called the dominant follicle. As the follicle grows, blood levels of estrogen rise significantly by cycle day seven. This increase in estrogen begins to inhibit the secretion of FSH. The fall in FSH allows smaller follicles to die off — they are, in effect, starved of FSH.",
  "cyA3FshL1": "FSH rises at the start of each cycle to kickstart follicle growth.",
  "cyA3FshL2": "Only the strongest follicle survives the natural FSH drop — all others are reabsorbed.",
  "cyA3FshL3": "FSH also induces the development of LH receptors within the dominant follicle, preparing it for the next step — ovulation.",
  "cyA3FshL4": "Abnormally high FSH levels can indicate reduced ovarian reserve — something doctors check during fertility investigations.",
  "cyA3LhH": "Hormone 2 — LH (Luteinizing Hormone)",
  "cyA3LhMeta": "Produced by: The pituitary gland\nPrimary role: Triggering ovulation and supporting the corpus luteum",
  "cyA3LhP1": "LH is the hormone responsible for the single most important event in the cycle: the release of the egg.",
  "cyA3LhP2": "Levels of estrogen, particularly estradiol, increase exponentially in the late follicular phase. When high levels of estradiol trigger LH secretion by gonadotropes — a positive feedback mechanism — this results in a massive LH surge, usually over 36 to 48 hours.",
  "cyA3LhP3": "The onset of the LH surge usually precedes ovulation by 36 hours. The peak of the LH surge precedes ovulation by 10 to 12 hours. The LH surge stimulates luteinization of the granulosa cells and stimulates the synthesis of progesterone.",
  "cyA3LhP4": "This is why ovulation predictor kits (OPKs) work — they detect the LH surge in urine, giving you a 24 to 36 hour window of advance warning before ovulation occurs.",
  "cyA3LhP5": "After ovulation, LH's role shifts. After ovulation, the ruptured follicle forms a corpus luteum — a temporary endocrine gland — that produces high levels of progesterone. Progesterone blocks the release of FSH and helps prepare the uterine lining.",
  "cyA3LhL1": "The LH surge is the direct trigger for ovulation.",
  "cyA3LhL2": "It can be detected in urine using OPK tests 24 to 36 hours before egg release.",
  "cyA3LhL3": "After ovulation, LH maintains the corpus luteum and its progesterone production.",
  "cyA3LhL4": "If no pregnancy occurs, LH drops, the corpus luteum dies, and the cycle resets.",
  "cyA3EstH": "Hormone 3 — Estrogen (Estradiol)",
  "cyA3EstMeta": "Produced by: The ovarian follicles, and later the corpus luteum\nPrimary role: Building uterine lining and acting as a powerful brain modulator",
  "cyA3EstP1": "Estrogen is the hormone most people have heard of, but its effects are far broader than most people realise. It does not just prepare the uterus — it acts directly on the brain, influencing mood, cognition, and emotional regulation.",
  "cyA3EstP2": "During the follicular phase, rising estrogen levels increase serotonin synthesis, enhancing mood, cognition, and pain tolerance. Estrogen may also influence dopamine levels, promoting motivation and reward sensitivity. GABA, involved in anxiety regulation, may be modulated by estrogen, inducing relaxation.",
  "cyA3EstP3": "A landmark 2024 study published in Frontiers in Neuroscience confirmed that estradiol functions as a neuroactive steroid, playing a crucial role in modulating neurotransmitter systems affecting neuronal circuits and brain functions including learning, memory, reward, and social behaviour.",
  "cyA3EstP4": "A separate study published in Nature Neuroscience in 2025 found that estrogen boosts dopamine-driven learning signals in the brain — providing a biological reason behind why motivation, focus, and mental clarity naturally ebb and flow throughout the month.",
  "cyA3EstBodyLabel": "What rising estrogen does to your body:",
  "cyA3EstBody1": "Thickens and builds the uterine lining (endometrium).",
  "cyA3EstBody2": "Changes cervical mucus from thick and sticky to clear and stretchy.",
  "cyA3EstBody3": "Improves skin clarity in the follicular phase.",
  "cyA3EstBody4": "Increases bone density and cardiovascular protection over time.",
  "cyA3EstBrainLabel": "What rising estrogen does to your brain:",
  "cyA3EstBrain1": "Boosts serotonin production — the neurotransmitter most associated with stable mood.",
  "cyA3EstBrain2": "Increases dopamine sensitivity — driving motivation, confidence, and reward-seeking.",
  "cyA3EstBrain3": "Enhances verbal memory, verbal fluency, and creative thinking.",
  "cyA3EstBrain4": "Reduces anxiety through its modulation of GABA.",
  "cyA3EstKey1": "Estrogen peaks twice: once just before ovulation (its highest point) and once mid-luteal phase.",
  "cyA3EstKey2": "The follicular phase energy and mood boost most people feel is directly caused by rising estrogen.",
  "cyA3EstKey3": "During the luteal phase, where estrogen levels are lower and progesterone levels are high, there is a corresponding decrease in serotonin levels — which is the neurobiological mechanism behind PMS mood symptoms.",
  "cyA3EstKey4": "Estrogen acts on over 300 different tissues in the body — it is not just a reproductive hormone.",
  "cyA3ProgH": "Hormone 4 — Progesterone",
  "cyA3ProgMeta": "Produced by: The corpus luteum\nPrimary role: Maintaining uterine lining and producing most PMS symptoms",
  "cyA3ProgP1": "Progesterone is the dominant hormone of the luteal phase and the hormone most responsible for the physical and emotional symptoms that most people associate with the days before their period.",
  "cyA3ProgP2": "After ovulation, the empty follicle transforms into the corpus luteum, which produces progesterone and some estrogen to support a potential pregnancy. The luteal phase is characterised by changes to hormone levels including a dramatic increase in progesterone, a decrease in FSH and LH, and changes to the endometrial lining.",
  "cyA3ProgP3": "Progesterone's effect on the brain is significant and often misunderstood. It does not simply cause bad moods — it acts on GABA receptors, the same receptors targeted by anti-anxiety medications. Progesterone and its metabolites interact with the GABAergic system, producing calming and sedating effects — which explains the fatigue and low motivation many people feel in the luteal phase.",
  "cyA3ProgP4": "The problem occurs at the end of the luteal phase, when both progesterone and estrogen drop sharply. A 2023 study published in Biological Psychiatry found an 18% change in serotonin transporter density in the midbrain between the periovulatory and premenstrual phase — directly correlating with the severity of depressed mood premenstrually.",
  "cyA3ProgBodyLabel": "What progesterone does to your body:",
  "cyA3ProgBody1": "Maintains and matures the uterine lining for a possible pregnancy.",
  "cyA3ProgBody2": "Increases basal body temperature slightly after ovulation.",
  "cyA3ProgBody3": "Causes water retention and bloating.",
  "cyA3ProgBody4": "Increases sebum production — often causing pre-period breakouts.",
  "cyA3ProgBody5": "Raises appetite, particularly for carbohydrates and fats.",
  "cyA3ProgBrainLabel": "What progesterone does to your brain:",
  "cyA3ProgBrain1": "Acts on GABA receptors, producing sedation and fatigue.",
  "cyA3ProgBrain2": "Reduces serotonin availability — contributing to low mood, irritability, and sensitivity.",
  "cyA3ProgBrain3": "Interacts with the dopaminergic system in complex ways — with varying effects on executive function.",
  "cyA3ProgBrain4": "Drives carbohydrate cravings through its influence on serotonin precursors.",
  "cyA3ProgKey1": "Progesterone is not the villain — it is doing its biological job correctly.",
  "cyA3ProgKey2": "PMS symptoms are the result of your brain's sensitivity to progesterone's effects on neurotransmitters, not a hormonal imbalance per se.",
  "cyA3ProgKey3": "The drop in progesterone at the end of the cycle is what triggers menstruation.",
  "cyA3ProgKey4": "If the corpus luteum persists (due to pregnancy), progesterone stays high — this is why periods stop during pregnancy.",
  "cyA3FbH": "How it all connects — the feedback loop",
  "cyA3FbP1": "The four hormones do not operate independently. They regulate each other through a continuous feedback system:",
  "cyA3FbL1": "Low estrogen at cycle start → hypothalamus releases GnRH → pituitary releases FSH and LH.",
  "cyA3FbL2": "FSH stimulates follicle growth → follicles produce estrogen → estrogen rises.",
  "cyA3FbL3": "High estrogen triggers LH surge → LH triggers ovulation.",
  "cyA3FbL4": "After ovulation, corpus luteum produces progesterone → progesterone and estrogen suppress FSH and LH.",
  "cyA3FbL5": "If no pregnancy: corpus luteum breaks down → progesterone and estrogen drop → FSH begins rising again → cycle restarts.",
  "cyA3FbP2": "Both estradiol and progesterone are secreted into the bloodstream and affect various tissues, including the uterus and pituitary gland. At the anterior pituitary, these hormones provide negative feedback, reducing the secretion of FSH and LH, which subsequently reduces their own production.",
  "cyA3StressH": "Why stress, sleep, and food affect your hormones",
  "cyA3StressP1": "The hormonal command chain starts in the hypothalamus — and the hypothalamus is directly connected to the brain's stress response system. This is why life circumstances can directly disrupt your cycle.",
  "cyA3StressP2": "Chronic stress and dysregulation of the HPA (hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal) axis can lead to alterations in cortisol levels, which are linked to both menstrual disorders and mood disorders. Elevated cortisol can suppress the release of GnRH, interfering with FSH and LH production and disrupting the entire hormonal cascade.",
  "cyA3StressP3": "The same mechanism explains why extreme caloric restriction, overtraining, illness, and significant weight changes can delay or stop ovulation — the hypothalamus detects physiological stress and downregulates the reproductive system as a protective response. Your body is not malfunctioning. It is prioritising survival.",
  "cyA3DocH": "When should you talk to a doctor?",
  "cyA3DocL1": "You have severe mood symptoms in the 1 to 2 weeks before your period that significantly affect your daily life (possible PMDD).",
  "cyA3DocL2": "Your periods are consistently absent.",
  "cyA3DocL3": "You experience hot flashes, night sweats, or significant mood changes unrelated to your cycle.",
  "cyA3DocL4": "You have been diagnosed with depression or anxiety and notice symptoms worsening significantly in the premenstrual phase.",
